<section class="sleep-chapter" id="prologue">
<p>Nine shadows haunt me.</p>
<p>Fuck it, these nine shadows can never be washed away, even after how much alcohol I drink, or how hard I would slap myself. Not even these drugs can help.</p>
<p>The first shadow and the last blooms darker than the rest, and the first...</p>
<p>...was my childhood friend.</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap1">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">The First</h2>
<p>Years ago, when my life was simple, the city and all its bluster, and the dreams I dream of achieving yet to bloom in my mind.</p>
{#
<p>I live in a small city, with a close-knit family.</p>
<p>And there she was, the First. She was my childhood friend, running towards me giddily as she excitedly said,</p>
<p><i>"I look for you everywhere, Al!"</i></p>
<p>I laughed as she jumps around me and pulled me on, walking towards a nearby park, our playground. <i>"Mum want me to eat first."</i></p>
<p><i>"Mrs. Al loves to feed, huh..."</i></p>
<p><i>"You should know, she loves it when you come and eat! No one ever clean plates faster than you!"</i></p>
#}
<p>In this hometown of mine, I had a friend. You can say she's a childhood friend.</p>
<p>Not a day goes by that all our other friends would tease us together. Sticking together for such time, there has to be love that blooms inside in the meantime.</p>
<p>We were kids back then. We don't get to talk about our relationship, especially love. It was just another funny teasing that never stops.</p>
<p>But time never stops. It brought us both towards our teenage years.</p>
<p>Shadows flit by and go away, but this childhood friend of mine remains the biggest shadow of them all. When we both reached high school age, our friendship began to be disturbed by expectations and false appearances.</p>
<p>High school. It was a time that I can never forget. I didn't think of it that much at the time, but now that I remember, I can't help but groan.</p>
<p>We both have moved to The City, the capital, at different times. She left first while we were still in Junior High School, and we met again in High School after three years.</p>
<p>I was surprised to see her appearance now. She's no longer the same person I knew. From her looks to her gait, even the way she speaks. She wore her long dark hair loose, her school costume was tighter than the school rules allowed it to be.</p>
<p>And she was with many of her friends, who share the same style, so to speak.</p>
<p>I approached her during my first days, intending to befriend her again. Those eyes do not even look at me, almost like she doesn't recognize that I exist. Like she never knew me at all.</p>
<p>Life didn't go that well for me after that after I realized something. It doesn't take that long for people to change, it doesn't take long for me to understand just how crude this world can get.</p>
<p>But other shadows waited for me.</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap2">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">The Second</h2>
<p>The Second was my cousin, my older cousin.</p>
<p>I shared a school with her during my Junior High School, and yes, it's another woman. I swear, not all of my shadows are feminine in nature.</p>
<p>We only have a difference of one year in age, and I often say hi to her when we pass by.</p>
<p>But we're not that close, to begin with. I only know her after meeting her at a regular family gathering. Her father is my uncle, my mother's younger brother.</p>
<p>One day, something changed between us.</p>
<p>I was greeting her like usual, but it's not a kind face or a greeting back that I get in return. She was silent instead, and she looked worried.</p>
<p>The next thing I knew, I was accosted by two students, the same year as me. I know these two in particular, friends of a particularly annoying guy in my class. A famous student, not just because of his looks, but because of his wealth.</p>
<p>They didn't get into my face about the whole thing, though.</p>
<p><i>"Hey... so here's the thing."</i> They began explaining. The famous guy is dating someone now.</p>
<p>I was confused. "What does that have to do with me?"</p>
<p>Then they revealed that my cousin was the one he was dating.</p>
<p>I was asked nicely to distance myself away from her. Very nicely.</p>
<p>"Where can I find this guy?" I was going to yell it out, but I kept myself calm and asked just as calmly as they were doing to me.</p>
<p>They answered, not knowing what I was about to do. I took off, my heart almost leaping out of my chest. In anger? I couldn't tell.</p>
<p>As soon as I saw his ugly face in the distance, I began to run. I heard the footsteps of his cronies behind me, following me after I just left them behind.</p>
<p>It was too late for them to stop me. My fist flew to his cheeks, and he ate it like it was his breakfast for the day.</p>
<p>That's not the only thing I gave him.</p>
<p>It was a full-fledged fight. Turns out, he's not all looks, he can dish some as well. We were both wounded before someone stopped the both of us from continuing.</p>
<p>I remember that in the middle of the fight, we were aggressively yelling at each other. I said that I will not bend my knee to his stupid ass, and he didn't reply, just defending himself and punching me in anger.</p>
<p>At some point, I do remember telling this coward that she's actually my family, my cousin.</p>
<p>We were both called to the principal's office, and we're both reprimanded, suspended, and forced to shake our hands in order to "make up".</p>
<p>After everything is over, and we were out of the office, my cousin approached us. She gets worried over her boyfriend's state, and then she looked at me.</p>
<p>I never knew that such soft hands can produce quite the slap.</p>
<p>The slap was loud enough that it echoes off the walls, and it was loud enough that other students stop and stare at us.</p>
<p>The look she gave me that day is still rattling around in my mind, even now. My cousin never forgave me, and we never spoke again with each other afterwards.</p>
<p>Even when later on she realized that her boyfriend was not a good guy after all, and they split up, we never made up.</p>
<p>Every family gathering afterwards is filled with awkwardness, and my mother stopped asking me to join them at some point.</p>
<p>The shadows line up, waiting for me to remember them.</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap3">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">The Third</h2>
<p>The Third is my best friend at the time, a boy.</p>
<p>Not long after I got slapped by the Second, I was no longer able to socialize with people in my school, not without them looking at me with fear and talking behind my back.</p>
<p>Only one boy was able to treat me normally.</p>
<p>We have been friends since the orientation period, at the beginning of our Junior High School era. He knows me well enough, and I believe that I know him just as well.</p>
<p>We often went on hangouts near a PC rental place, or an internet cafe after school, or played soccer together, or playing anything else.</p>
<p>Playing with him felt the same as playing with my old childhood friend, the First. He almost feels like a replacement of sorts.</p>
<p>After the incident, there was no change in our friendship. We still talk with each other, and we even discuss about the fight. I explained only half of the reality of the situation, the part that I'm comfortable telling.</p>
<p>Maybe I should've been more clear to him, or maybe that won't change a thing.</p>
<p>Soon after, I can feel him distancing away from me. Our usual routine of playing breaks, and one day it even stops.</p>
<p>We rarely even talk after a while.</p>
<p>One day, when I had enough of the judging eyes people were giving me, I noticed him talking with one of the cronies of that coward.</p>
<p>I heard the conversation. <i>"You know him, he's not a good guy..."</i> Just another voice in the sea of people talking about my temper. I almost got myself mad again and wanted to confront him, but I stopped myself. I promised that I wouldn't fight again.</p>
<p>I stood still and listened on. <i>"Yeah, I mean... I didn't know he was like that..."</i> Turns out he already planned to distance himself away from me after the incident. <i>"He's too aggressive..."</i></p>
<p>Usually in stories like this, the characters would say that they feel like time stopped for them when they got betrayed like this, but not for me.</p>
<p>The only person I could trust, that I think could understand me...</p>
<p>Without thinking, I moved myself to face them. I stare straight into my friend's eyes, and say,<i>"You don't need to be friends with me anymore if you feel like you're forced to be. Thank you for everything."</i></p>
<p>And I learned to be alone since then, learned to survive against those who judged me for what I did.</p>
<p>One year later, my Junior High School era ended, and those shadows I left behind as my father moved away to the Capital for work.</p>
<p>A bright shadow awaits me.</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap4">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">The Fourth</h2>
<p>The Fourth is my biggest regret. I do not want her to be my shadow, and as a result, she is the "brightest" shadow out of all, because deep down I wish we can meet again in the distant future.</p>
<p>She is a dear friend, different from the rest, and she is the only one I can truly call friend from the deepest part of my heart.</p>
<p>During my High School era, I managed to get in to a prestigious international high-school, tying in to my father's upward trajectory in his job.</p>
<p>And in this school, they have a student exchange program with foreign high school all across the world.</p>
<p>In this particular language class, I was paired with her. Contrary to my expectations, she can actually speak my language, albeit not as fluently. I also can speak her foreign language, and perhaps I was also not as fluent. We became partners just for that particular class, at first.</p>
<p>After weeks of classes went by, at some point we started exchanging contacts with each other.</p>
<p>We started corresponding with each other regularly, speaking in each other's language, and correcting each other when we were wrong. We also started to talk freely about other topics outside of school subjects.</p>
<p>Our communication gets clearer as time goes on. I can speak her language without stuttering, and she can speak in my language fluently, without any hint of an accent.</p>
<p>One day, out of the blue, she asked me out to eat somewhere.</p>
<p>At that age, I wasn't as oblivious as to not understand the implication of this request, but I agreed to meet up anyway.</p>
<p>We both meet up at a coffee shop, and at first, there was some awkwardness. This is our first time meeting up outside of school anyway, and if I can guess, she also realizes what it means to meet up with just the two of us. But, as soon as we started talking, all those awkwardness seems to melt away.</p>
<p>After that meetup, we would consistently have one every week, meeting in other places like a restaurant, even going to the mall, just the two of us. We both never really talk about what we are, but we enjoy each other's company.</p>
<p>Our time together didn't last long.</p>
<p>It seems like her father, just like mine, work in the type of job that requires them to move around, and her father has been transferred to work in another country.</p>
<p>She has told me about her father before when we hang out. It seems like she's used to it, as her family has to move every so often to a new country.She also confessed that she already asked his father to stay for an extra year in this country.</p>
<p><i>"Why?"</i> I asked, confused about the sudden request.</p>
<p><i>"Well, I... found someone I can't really leave behind."</i></p>
<p>Her cheeks reddened as she looks away. It took me a few seconds before I realized what she meant, and I also look away for a bit. At some point, our eyes meet, and seconds that felt like infinity passed away.</p>
<p>We both laughed, and then continue on to another topic.</p>
<p>The moment has come. She is leaving, and she invited me to see her off in the airport. I meet her family, her father, her mother, and her little sister. I shyly try to speak in their language, and they understand and smiled at my attempt.</p>
<p>I spoke to each of her family, until the moment comes when I face her. Our eyes once again meet. I can see her cheeks puffy as if she has been sobbing all night. I wasn't sure what my face look like, and I would like to think that I've wiped away all the evidences that I cried myself to sleep last night.</p>
<p>"Hey-" The words I speak are interrupted as she came barrelling forward and wrapped her arms around my neck. Have I ever told you that our heights are not that far off each other? I have been told by other people that I am pretty tall amongst the boys in my school before, but she almost matched me in height.</p>
<p>"We have to see each other again." She said, still hugging me. I can hear the hint of her sob between each words, which made me start shedding a tear of my own. "Promise me."</p>
<p>"I promise." I croaked out. "Either I come to meet you..."</p>
<p>"Or I come visit you." She finished my words, and she let me go as she look me closely. "I promise." She gives a wide smile, the kind of smile that you can't help but smile back at, which I did.</p>
<p>"Are we too old for pinky promise?" I said half-jokingly.</p>
<p>"Yeah... but what the hell." She giggled as she pulled out her pinky. I sighed and smiled as I pulled out mine and intertwined it with hers.</p>
<p>We reluctantly let go, and I can never forget the look on her face. It was the brightest smile you can ever muster, and she is still shedding her tears.</p>
<p>"I don't wanna say goodbye, cause it'll be too awkward when we meet again. See you!" She then turns her back and follow her family, already far away in a hurry to get to their flight.</p>
<p>And so the plane flew away from this country.</p>
<p>I cannot contact her for the longest time, even until now... but I know where she is.</p>
<p>The shadow seems dimmer than the rest, because it's filled with hope that one day I get to meet her again.</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap5">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">The Fifth</h2>
<p>The Fifth is a friend I made during highschool, right after The Fourth left.</p>
<p>If I was given a choice, I would choose to not meet this person, this shadow.</p>
<p>But during this time, especially after she left, my other option were to be a loner, and ends up being a shadow of my own self.</p>
<p>And so I made the option to befriend someone.</p>
<p>It was just a chance meeting, as we entered the second year. The students gets mixed around as we advanced another grade, and he happens to be my seatmates, sitting in the back of the class.</p>
<p>We bonded over games and talking about sports... and sometimes talking about our classmates. From our talk, I figured out that this friend of mine also has the same thought process as me. For us to survive during this period, we needed someone to back us up.</p>
<p>He became the person I rely on to remind me of homework, as I do the same to him if he ever forgets. We eat lunch together, and it seems like he doesn't have any other friends other than me.</p>
<p>But I still hold myself back. I was already betrayed once, there's no way in hell I will get caught with my pants down again.</p>
<p>This way, with a clear head, I was able to see the betrayal way before it happens.</p>
<p>In the end of my second year, I found out that this friend of mine has talked shit behind my back to other students, the infamous ones. Apparently, this infamous group of students spread a rumor around that I was responsible for how hard it is to approach the Fourth, and this friend joins in on discussing, and falsely confirming the rumor. It seems like he sensed an opportunity to be a part of them and seized it.</p>
<p>I happened to overhear him talk about me as I went home for the last time during that second year. I didn't get enraged at the time. A particular memory keeps popping up every time my emotion balls up, of a familiar face, and the phantom feeling on my arms. I managed to calm myself. There's no need to be as aggressive as before.</p>
<p>Just like before, though, they are all cowards. The only thing differentiating them is their numbers far overwhelm those I faced before. And this friend of mine wanted to join, perhaps as a self-preservation before he gets bullied himself.</p>
<p>They're only talking behind my back, however. There's nothing else going on, at least, that's what I think at first.</p>
<p>Because not long after that, the sixth shadow emerges because I didn't act on my feelings.</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap6">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">The Sixth</h2>
<p>At the first day of the last year of my high school, I accidentally bumped someone as I park my bicycle.</p>
<p>The person I bumped is my sixth shadow. Out of all the shadows I have, he is the one I hate the most, because he is the sole reason why I left high school with a sour taste.</p>
<p>This boy doesn't take well to my accidental mistake, and his personality reminded me of the coward back at my hometown.</p>
<p>Unlike that person, however, he is far richer and much more psychotic. He happened to be one of the lynchpin of the infamous students that I previously mentioned, the one that just so happened to spread rumors about me and the Fourth to everyone.</p>
<p>That accident became the catalyst of a year-long game of cat and mouse between me and his posse, which includes the Fifth. I can see however that his heart is not in it. He can still feel guilt after all.</p>
<p>After that moment, the whole school became our playground of hide and seek for me and him. My peaceful school life hinges on my ability to hide before the group can find me and rough me up.</p>
<p>I was only caught unaware the first time they decided to rough me up. After that, I deftly able to dodge them and found myself a safe space somewhere in school, an abandoned room that no one check out for some reason.</p>
<p>The whole game lasted right until I graduate, but I know for a fact that this "game" will continue to haunt me for the rest of my life. This boy's grudge... no, this <i>bastard's</i> grudge won't be satiated. It will never die down.</p>
<p>Well... maybe it was because I kind of pranked them back at times, getting them in trouble with the teachers, and even tinkered with their vehicles.</p>
<p>I laughed hollowly everytime I remember those times. Most people would have nostalgic memories of their high school, but I only remember the time when I have to fight to survive every day.</p>
<p>There's no one there to help me. No mysterious power that made me a main character. No one in my corner... at least, that's what I think at the time.</p>
<p>One day, in my attempts to avoid them, I fell on my knees and have to hobble away to my safe hiding spot. As I rest, I realized that I badly hurt my knees, and I just sit there helplessly, holding on to tears of pain and suffering. A whole year avoiding bullies, that's my life for the past year. Pathetic.</p>
<p>But then I can hear someone approaching. I almost stood up, panicking, thinking of an escape route. If they can find me here out of all places...</p>
<p>When that long black hair flows freely into my vision, and that shadow I left behind in my hometown and my broken thoughts appear, I was shocked. What is she doing here?</p>
<p>It was the First. Isn't it enough that she blew me off years ago, when I first approached her? Isn't it enough that she become the school diva, with everyone pining for her? Isn't it enough that this girl happens to date the person responsible for my whole year of suffering?</p>
<p>And now she just waltz here... with a first aid box in her hands. "Sit," she said as she sat down in front of me, guiding me to follow her suit. I was taken aback by her request, all of my hateful questions suddenly disappear like a puff of smoke. I nodded obidiently and sit down.</p>
<p>She started treating my wound while I grimaced in pain. As she cleans up my wound, all of the words left unsaid came out, as I can no longer hold them back.</p>
<p>"So, <i>now</i> you recognize me?"</p>
<p>I was ready to tell her off, but then our eyes meet. I can see guilt beneath her eyes, and I mustered a look that made it clear what I feel about her actions. Betrayed.</p>
<p>"Yeah," she said curtly.</p>
<p>"Where have you been?"</p>
<p>"I never went anywhere."</p>
<p>"Why... why now?"</p>
<p>She went silent, and then continue to clean up my wound as she started to cover it with bandages. She doesn't say anything while doing so.</p>
<p>I was about to continue but I was interrupted.</p>
<p>"I can't keep up the charades anymore." She said softly, wading through the silence that blanketed us both after I asked her why. I was stunned to silence by her confession.</p>
<p>"I'm... I'm sorry for pretending to not know you all these years." She said, as she finished tending to my wound and took all of the bandages and antiseptic in the first aid box. She stood up quickly without saying anything, and began to walk away.</p>
<p>I grit my teeth and stood up as well, withstanding the pain, and stops her, holding her shoulder. "Why do you have to pretend?" The words just came out, along with the tears that I've been holding back ever since she ignored me.</p>
<p>She doesn't immediately turn to look to me, but once she did, I also noticed tears rolling down her cheek. "I also have to survive here."</p>
<p>She then walked away from me, my hands reaching up to her empty visage.</p>
<p>After that, the First's shadow grew bigger, even more so after this meeting. She still pretended to not know me... but I know now that she still cared.</p>
<p>It doesn't help me at all to survive, so her caring doesn't mean shit to me... but it does settle my heart a little bit. It's not something that I did wrong. I had made it up in my mind that she was avoiding me because of something that I did wrong.</p>
<p>And with that, my high school time is over. This "game" continues even until the very last day, but they weren't able to do anything during the graduation since my parents (and their parents) are present. At least, my graduation memory is not tainted by this.</p>
<p>Maybe I should've at least told my parents about this. But they don't need to know. They will never know.</p>
<p>Because they are the Seventh and the Eighth shadows. The only shadow that haunted me from the day I was born, and perhaps, until they day I die.</p>
</section>

